4. Architectural Principles
4.1 Relation-Driven Design

Foresy follows the following mandatory architecture rule:

No business entity owns a foreign key directly to another business entity.

Relationships between business domains are represented through explicit relation entities/tables.

Therefore:

User
  │
  │
  ▼
UserCompany
  │
  │
  ▼
Company

UserCompany is the explicit relationship between User and Company.

4.2 Foreign Keys on Relation Tables

The Relation-Driven principle does not prohibit a relation table from referencing the entities it relates.

Therefore:

user_companies.user_id    → users.id
user_companies.company_id → companies.id

is valid and required.

What is prohibited is:

users.company_id
companies.user_id

or any equivalent direct cross-domain ownership.

4.3 No default_scope

No default_scope may be introduced.

Lifecycle behavior must use explicit scopes.

Example:

Company.active
Company.deleted
5. User Without Company

A User may exist without any Company.

This is a mandatory product requirement.

The following state is valid:

User
 └── no UserCompany

This allows onboarding and simulation before company creation.

No fictitious Company must be created to represent this situation.

6. No Fictitious Company

FC-08 MUST NOT create a fake, demo, placeholder, or fictitious Company to represent a User who has no real Company.

Simulation data must remain conceptually distinct from real Company data.

A simulation is not a Company.

A future feature may provide a mechanism to transform simulation/planning data into real activity after Company creation, but that functionality is outside FC-08.

7. Company Definition

A Company represents a legal or organizational entity through which activity may be conducted.

At FC-08 level, Company contains administrative and legal information.

Company does not contain:

user_id
User role
Mission references
CRA references
Client references
Independent references

Those relationships belong to dedicated relation structures.

8. UserCompany Definition

UserCompany represents the relationship between a User and a Company.

It answers:

"How does this User act in relation to this Company?"

The relationship contains a role.

The relationship is therefore contextual rather than global.

9. Supported Roles

FC-08 supports the following roles:

9.1 independent

The User acts as an Independent through the Company.

This role will support future features involving:

personal activity
Missions performed by the Independent
CRA
revenue
regulatory and fiscal simulation
9.2 client

The User acts as a Client / representative of the Company.

This role will support future features involving:

engaging an Independent
client-side Missions
projects where an Independent represents the external party
costs/charges associated with engaging an Independent

The implementation must not assume that a User can only ever be an Independent.

10. Multiple Roles

A User may have multiple roles with the same Company.

Example:

User A
 ├── Company X / independent
 └── Company X / client

This is valid.

There is no global uniqueness constraint on:

(user_id, company_id)

The uniqueness constraint is instead:

(user_id, company_id, role)

Therefore:

User A + Company X + independent
User A + Company X + client

may coexist.

But:

User A + Company X + independent
User A + Company X + independent

must not coexist.

11. Multiple Companies

A User may have relationships with multiple Companies.

Example:

User A
 ├── Company 1 / independent
 ├── Company 2 / client
 └── Company 3 / independent

FC-08 does not impose a global maximum number of Companies or relationships.

Any future regulatory constraint must be implemented in the domain feature where it becomes relevant and must not be encoded prematurely in FC-08.

12. Role Is Relationship Context

A User does not have a single persistent role such as:

User.role = independent

Instead:

UserCompany.role = independent

or:

UserCompany.role = client

This allows the same User to act differently depending on the Company.

13. Activity Separation

A User acting as:

Company A / independent

and the same User acting as:

Company A / client

does not represent the same business activity.

Future Missions and CRA structures must therefore preserve this distinction.

FC-08 does not implement that activity model.

14. Mission Consequence

A future Mission must be understood within a specific business activity context.

The following are conceptually different:

User → Company A → Independent → Mission X

and:

User → Company A → Client → Mission Y

Even when:

the client is the same;
the Independent is the same;
the TJM is the same;
the Mission type is the same;
the commercial context appears identical.

The activities remain distinct.

Mission duplication or explicit activity separation will be addressed in the appropriate future Feature Contract.

15. CRA Boundary

FC-07 established the CRA as a source of truth for realized activity.

FC-08 does not modify FC-07.

The following concepts are explicitly outside FC-08:

CRA forecast mechanics
automatic forecast → realized conversion
monthly CRA timeline behavior
CRA validation workflow changes
scenario/simulation storage
transformation of simulation into forecast
transformation of simulation into realized activity

These must be specified in a future Feature Contract.

16. Forecast / Realized / Scenario Boundary

Foresy must eventually distinguish:

REALIZED
FORECAST
SCENARIO

Conceptually:

REALIZED represents actual activity;
FORECAST represents expected activity;
SCENARIO represents hypothetical activity.

FC-08 does not implement this model.

The Company model must nevertheless remain compatible with its future introduction.

17. Company Lifecycle

Company lifecycle uses soft deletion.

The lifecycle field is:

deleted_at

No status or archived_at field is introduced by FC-08.

Active Company
deleted_at IS NULL
Deleted Company
deleted_at IS NOT NULL
