22. UserCompany Domain Model

UserCompany uses its existing UUID primary key.

The primary key MUST remain UUID.

Field	PostgreSQL Type	Null	Default	Constraint / Meaning
id	uuid	No	existing UUID generation	Primary key
user_id	bigint	No	—	FK to users.id
company_id	uuid	No	—	FK to companies.id
role	PostgreSQL enum	No	—	User's role with Company
deleted_at	datetime	Yes	—	Soft deletion
created_at	datetime	No	—	Rails timestamp
updated_at	datetime	No	—	Rails timestamp
23. UserCompany Foreign Keys

Because UserCompany is the explicit relationship table, it may reference both entities.

Required relationships:

user_companies.user_id
    → users.id

and:

user_companies.company_id
    → companies.id

The types must match the actual referenced primary keys:

users.id    = bigint
companies.id = uuid

Therefore:

user_companies.user_id    = bigint
user_companies.company_id = uuid

No conversion to UUID or bigint is permitted where it would contradict the existing referenced PK.

24. UserCompany Role Type

The existing PostgreSQL enum:

user_company_role_enum

must be retained.

The logical domain value remains string-like:

independent
client

but the PostgreSQL storage type is the existing enum.

No rollback to an unrestricted string column is permitted.

25. UserCompany Role Rules

Allowed roles for FC-08:

independent
client

Any additional roles are future extensions and must be introduced through a dedicated Feature Contract.

26. UserCompany Uniqueness

The database must enforce:

UNIQUE(user_id, company_id, role)

This permits:

(user A, company X, independent)
(user A, company X, client)

but rejects:

(user A, company X, independent)
(user A, company X, independent)
27. UserCompany Lifecycle

UserCompany also supports soft deletion through:

deleted_at

Deletion must not physically destroy historical relationship information.

The relationship can therefore be retained for audit purposes.

Re-activation

A deleted UserCompany must not be implicitly resurrected by creating an identical active relationship.

Reactivation, if required, must be an explicit operation defined by a future contract.

28. Relationship History

Soft deletion provides a first level of historical preservation.

FC-08 does not introduce:

version tables;
event sourcing;
temporal validity ranges;
valid_from;
valid_until;
audit event streams.

Those mechanisms may be introduced later if required.

29. Company Scopes

The Company model must expose explicit scopes:

Company.active
Company.deleted

Expected semantics:

Company.active
→ deleted_at IS NULL
Company.deleted
→ deleted_at IS NOT NULL

No default_scope is allowed.

30. UserCompany Scopes

UserCompany should expose explicit lifecycle scopes consistent with Company:

UserCompany.active
UserCompany.deleted

No default_scope is allowed.

31. No Implicit Filtering

Controllers and services must not rely on implicit model filtering.

Every query requiring active records must explicitly request the appropriate scope.

Example:

current_user_companies = UserCompany.active.where(user_id: current_user.id)
