39. Role and Company Parameters

role belongs to the UserCompany relationship.

It is therefore not a Company attribute.

The API must preserve this distinction.

company.*
    → Company

role
    → UserCompany
40. wrap_parameters

The CompaniesController must support Rails parameter wrapping for flat JSON payloads.

The configuration must be equivalent to:

wrap_parameters :company, include: [
  :name,
  :siren,
  :siret,
  :legal_form,
  :tax_number,
  :address_line_1,
  :address_line_2,
  :postal_code,
  :city,
  :country,
  :currency,
  :vat_regime
]

role must NOT be included in the wrapper.

The controller must read:

params[:role]

separately.

41. Flat JSON Compatibility

The API must correctly process supported flat JSON requests through Rails parameter wrapping.

Example:

{
  "name": "Example Consulting",
  "siren": "123456789",
  "role": "independent"
}

The Company attributes are wrapped by Rails.

The role remains a relationship parameter.

42. Parameter Security

Only explicitly permitted Company attributes may be accepted.

The following must never be mass-assigned from the Company request:

id
deleted_at
created_at
updated_at
user_id

The relationship role must be separately validated and authorized.

43. Authorization

A User may access a Company only when an appropriate active UserCompany relationship exists.

Authorization must be explicit.

A User must not gain access to a Company merely by knowing its ID.

Cross-user access must return the standard unauthorized/forbidden API error according to the existing Foresy authorization contract.

44. Standardized API Errors

FC-08 must use the existing standardized API error contract.

The response shape is:

{
  "code": "ERROR_CODE",
  "message": "Human-readable message",
  "details": {}
}

FC-08 must not introduce an alternative error format.

Expected error categories include:

validation errors;
authentication errors;
authorization errors;
duplicate SIREN;
duplicate SIRET;
invalid SIREN;
invalid role;
missing required parameters;
resource not found.

Exact error codes must remain aligned with the existing application-wide error contract.

45. Business Validation Rules
45.1 SIREN

SIREN:

is mandatory;
must be valid;
must be unique.
45.2 SIRET

SIRET:

is optional;
if provided, must be valid according to the implemented format validation;
must be unique when non-null.
45.3 Company Name

Name is mandatory.

45.4 Role

Role is mandatory for the initial UserCompany relationship.

Role must be one of:

independent
client
45.5 Duplicate Relationship

An identical active relationship must not be created twice.

Database uniqueness remains authoritative.

46. Database Constraints

The following constraints are mandatory.

Company
PRIMARY KEY(id)
UNIQUE(siren)
UNIQUE(siret)

The unique SIRET index must permit multiple NULL values according to PostgreSQL semantics.

UserCompany
PRIMARY KEY(id)
FOREIGN KEY(user_id) → users.id
FOREIGN KEY(company_id) → companies.id
UNIQUE(user_id, company_id, role)
47. Sequential Migration Plan

The implementation must first inspect the actual current schema before executing migrations.

47.1 SIREN

Current state:

nullable
non-unique index

Target state:

NOT NULL
UNIQUE

The existing non-unique SIREN index must be replaced, not duplicated.

Migration sequence:

remove_index :companies, :siren
change_column_null :companies, :siren, false
add_index :companies, :siren, unique: true

Before changing nullability or uniqueness, existing data must be verified to satisfy the target constraints.

48. SIRET

Current state:

NOT NULL
UNIQUE

Target state:

NULL
UNIQUE when non-null

Migration:

change_column_null :companies, :siret, true

The existing unique index may remain.

PostgreSQL allows multiple NULL values in a standard unique index.

